    42	#     BEHAVIOUR STILL EXISTS IN THE TREE **AND IS STILL AN ACTIVE TEST** —
    43	#     carrying a `#[test]`-family attribute, and not disabled by `#[ignore]` or
    44	#     `#[cfg(..)]` (fix-5).
    45	#
    46	# It does NOT re-execute those behavioural suites, and it therefore does NOT
    47	# prove the behaviour is still correct — only that the proof has not been
    48	# deleted, renamed away, or SWITCHED OFF. `cargo test --workspace` is what
    49	# proves the behaviour; this gate is what notices when the proof disappears,
    50	# stops running, or when the contract's structural obligations are no longer met.
    51	#
    52	# So: it CATCHES a symbol/table/variant that was renamed or removed, a
    53	# forbidden symbol that reappeared, a named test that was deleted OR quietly
    54	# disabled, and any edit to the ratified contract. It DOES NOT catch a
    55	# behavioural regression inside a still-present, still-named, still-running
    56	# test, nor anything the Memex repo does. Claiming otherwise would be a false
    57	# assurance — which is the TC-37 failure class in a different costume.
    58	#
    59	# ===================== PROBE SCOPE — WHY THE ASYMMETRY =======================
    60	# fix-3, codex §9 round 3 findings #1 and #2 [P2]. Every NEGATIVE probe used to
    61	# read ONE FILE — a crate's lib.rs. So a LITERAL, correctly-spelled, fully
    62	# schema-qualified `CREATE VIRTUAL TABLE main.property_search_index ...` placed in
    63	# a NEW module beside lib.rs (codex's `fathomdb-engine/src/extra_fts.rs`) exited
    64	# 0, and so did an `INSERT INTO canonical_attributes ...` in a new schema-crate
    65	# module. That is not the residual scope below and not a regex-shape problem: it
    66	# is exactly what this gate claims to catch, in ordinary source text, missed
    67	# because the check was pointed at the wrong subject. The engine crate ALREADY
    68	# carries sibling modules (lifecycle.rs, pcache2.rs); "somebody adds a module" is
    69	# how a crate grows, not an evasion.
    70	#
    71	# THE RULE, AND WHY IT IS NOT SYMMETRIC:
    72	#
    73	#   * NEGATIVE probes (`absent_tree`) are TREE-scoped, always. A negative
    74	#     assertion scoped to one file is only as strong as the assumption that
    75	#     nobody adds a module, and it fails SILENTLY GREEN when that assumption
    76	#     breaks. There is no file-scoped negative probe kind in this script — the
    77	#     `("absent", <file>, regex)` kind was DELETED, not left unused, so writing
    78	#     one again is a deliberate, reviewed act rather than the path of least
    79	#     effort. scripts/tests/test_check_c1_conformance.sh asserts its absence.
    80	#
    81	#   * POSITIVE probes (`present`, `doc_text`) and the STRUCTURAL readers
    82	#     (`in_item`, `fn_sig`, `fn_defined`, `sql_ddl`, `fts_tokenizer_shared`,
    83	#     `enum_exact`, `arms_exact`) legitimately stay FILE-scoped. Their failure
    84	#     mode is the opposite one: a too-narrow positive scope cannot produce a
    85	#     false green, it produces a false RED by construction. If the symbol, the
    86	#     enum or the impl block MOVES to a sibling module, the probe finds 0 matches
    87	#     (or "could not locate a parseable enum") and the gate fails loudly, which
    88	#     is the safe side. Pinning them to a named file also states WHERE the
    89	#     contract's structure is expected to live, which is information a tree-wide
    90	#     search would throw away.
    91	#
    92	#   * A CRATE TREE IS THE COMPLETE SCOPE, not merely a wider one, for the Rust
    93	#     identifier negatives: a variant cannot be added to `enum DenseReadiness` or
    94	#     `enum ProjectionRole` from outside the crate that declares them.
    95	#
    96	# CURRENT INVENTORY (7 negative probes, all tree-scoped; 5 clauses):
    97	#   src                                     Q4-NO-PROVISIONAL (*.rs);
    98	#                                           Q6B-NO-ENTITYTYPESPEC ×3 (all text)
    99	#   fathomdb-schema/src                     Q2-NO-DATA-MIGRATION ×2 (all text)
   100	#   fathomdb-engine/src                     Q4-DENSE-READINESS ×1 (*.rs),
   101	#                                           Q6A-THREE-ROLES ×2 (*.rs),
   102	#                                           Q6B-ID-NON-NULL ×1 (*.rs),
   103	#                                           TE-CUSTOM-TOKENIZER ×1 (all text)
   104	# The SQL negatives use exts=None rather than *.rs on purpose: a migration or a
   105	# DDL carried in a `.sql` file and pulled in with `include_str!` is the same
   106	# violation. The Rust IDENTIFIER negatives use *.rs, because their subject is
   107	# Rust syntax and a mention in a neighbouring note would be a pure false RED.
   108	#
   109	# ==================== RESIDUAL SCOPE — READ BEFORE ROUND 6 ===================
   110	# This gate is a STATIC, LEXICAL check over Rust and SQL SOURCE TEXT. That
   111	# approach has a boundary, and two review rounds were spent tightening regexes
   112	# against it (fix-1: SQL case/whitespace, and blacklist ⇒ closed vocabulary;
   113	# fix-2: schema-qualified table names, and spellings admitted outside a simple
   114	# match arm). Rather than rediscover the boundary again, it is written down here.
   115	#
   116	# NOTE THAT FIX-3, FIX-4 AND FIX-5 ARE NOT ON THIS LIST AND DID NOT BELONG ON IT.
   117	# Round 3 was a bounded SCOPE bug (one file vs the crate tree), round 4 a bounded
   118	# SUBJECT-BINDING bug (a file vs the named table/struct/enum/function the clause
   119	# is about), and round 5 two more bounded ones — the WRONG CRATE'S same-named
   120	# symbol, and a test function that is no longer a test. All three had definite,
   121	# complete fixes and all three were CLOSED, not documented away. The list below
   122	# is the set of things a different MECHANISM would be needed for; it is not a
   123	# place to retire findings to.
   124	#
   125	# WHAT THIS GATE IS. A TRIPWIRE AGAINST DRIFT. Its threat model is a FUTURE
   126	# CONTRIBUTOR who changes as-built code without noticing that a ratified
   127	# cross-repo contract said otherwise — the ordinary way a `can-i-deploy`
   128	# precondition rots. It is NOT a proof of conformance and NOT an adversarial
   129	# control: it cannot stop someone deliberately smuggling a backfill past CI, and
   130	# nothing assembled out of regexes over source text could.
   131	#
   132	# THE CLASSES IT DOES NOT AND CANNOT CATCH, concretely:
   133	#
   134	#   1. DYNAMICALLY COMPOSED SQL. The probes read literal source text. A
   135	#      `format!("INSERT INTO {} SELECT ...", TABLE)`, a `push_str` built from
   136	#      fragments, or a table name held in a `const`/`static` all reach the
   137	#      forbidden table while the text this gate reads contains only `{}`.
   138	#
   139	#   2. ANOTHER ROUTE TO THE SAME TABLE. `qualified()` closes the SPELLINGS of
   140	#      the PINNED NAME (`main.canonical_attributes`, `` `main` . x ``, `[main].x`,
   141	#      any case, any whitespace). It does not close every ROUTE: an ATTACHed
   142	#      database whose alias is chosen at runtime, a VIEW or an `INSTEAD OF`
   143	#      TRIGGER that writes the table under a different name, or an
   144	#      `ALTER TABLE ... RENAME` are all invisible to it.
   145	#
   146	#   3. IDENTIFIER INDIRECTION IN RUST. `const PENDING: &str = "pending";` used
